# public_eval_b99e7126

A modal-color lattice separates equal-sized micro-tiles. One tile pattern is
ordinary and repeated most often; one anomalous tile introduces exactly one new
ink color. The anomalous tile's ink mask is also a macro-grid mask. Translate
that mask to the unique anchor covering every visible anomalous clue tile, then
copy the complete anomalous tile into every selected macro position.

The matched-input family keeps the faithful `all_ink` rule as its default and
adds `largest_component`, which segments the input-derived ink mask and retains
only its unique largest four-connected component before macro completion. The
first two demonstrations distinguish the complete mask from the component-only,
bounding-box, clue-only, rotated, transposed, and smallest-component alternatives.

Every episode stores four distinct queries, supports 2--6 demonstrations, and
reveals one independently shuffled query at a time. Settings, programs, query
answers, future queries, and source ordinals remain private. Owner registration,
qualification, and acceptance remain pending.
